(7291277, 1135834, 84661, '<td valign="top">Thanks to all who commented on this image. I had fun taking it.</td>', 'Thanks to all who commented on this image. I had fun taking it.', '2014-12-08 11:44:30.000000', NULL, 0),
(7296688, 1136153, 43463, '<td valign="top">Great composition and detail in this pattern architecture shot of the stairwell. I like what the fisheye lens does for the composition because it curves the normally straight edge objects it makes the imagery flow smoother in a swirl/whirlpool direction down towards the bottom floor. <br/> <br/>Just read your description on how you achieved the shot - running to activate the lights on the floors, now that is photographic lighting that provides not just light but a full workout:-)! Congrats on the Blue!</td>', 'Great composition and detail in this pattern architecture shot of the stairwell. I like what the fisheye lens does for the composition because it curves the normally straight edge objects it makes the imagery flow smoother in a swirl/whirlpool direction down towards the bottom floor.  Just read your description on how you achieved the shot - running to activate the lights on the floors, now that is photographic lighting that provides not just light but a full workout:-)! Congrats on the Blue!', '2014-12-29 17:21:40.000000', NULL, 0),
(7402099, 1135982, 25024, '<td valign="top">*Hello from Sid and the Critique Club*<br/> <br/>An intriguing image that meets the challenge.<br/> <br/>The theme of the challenge is met by the wall your friend is leaning against and that''s where the intrigue lies, it seems to consist of two different textures with the lower half giving a fascinating grainy appearance, unless this is your own partial processing? I love the stark high contrasts you have here and the shadows themselves are also quite interesting, they add another element to the scene.<br/> <br/>Assuming the sloping shadow continues upwards as implied I think I may have been tempted to move everything further over to the right revealing more of the slope and excluding the pole shadow on the right and lowered it to just above his hanging out shirt for just his torso. I think this would have improved the composition and therefore its impact and hopefully have scored higher. <br/> <br/>I do love the effect of the lower half of the wall. Nicely done, thanks for your submission and apologies for the delayed critique, as they say, ''better late than never'', or at least I hope it is, Sid</td>', '*Hello from Sid and the Critique Club* An intriguing image that meets the challenge. The theme of the challenge is met by the wall your friend is leaning against and that''s where the intrigue lies, it seems to consist of two different textures with the lower half giving a fascinating grainy appearance, unless this is your own partial processing? I love the stark high contrasts you have here and the shadows themselves are also quite interesting, they add another element to the scene. Assuming the sloping shadow continues upwards as implied I think I may have been tempted to move everything further over to the right revealing more of the slope and excluding the pole shadow on the right and lowered it to just above his hanging out shirt for just his torso. I think this would have improved the composition and therefore its impact and hopefully have scored higher.  I do love the effect of the lower half of the wall. Nicely done, thanks for your submission and apologies for the delayed critique, as they say, ''better late than never'', or at least I hope it is, Sid', '2015-09-03 09:23:47.000000', NULL, 0),
(7402102, 1135832, 25024, '<td valign="top">*Hello from Sid and the Critique Club*<br/> <br/>An interesting image that fulfils the challenge perfectly.<br/> <br/>There can be no mistake that this most certainly is a huge lump of concrete, but not just any old concrete, it is a very important part of recent history. It is brought alive by the colourful graffiti and your title during the challenge and post challenge with your very helpful comments.<br/> <br/>I know one of your commenters likes the inclusion of the background, I am not so keen, or rather I love the irregular left edge of the concrete and this should be preserved by the inclusion of a minimal background, I think where the brown structure becomes black would have been the ideal amount to include and I would have dodged or cloned the part of the canopy that would intrude to make it more uniform. The bright lights of bokeh in the window are very distracting and spoil the overall result especially as we are excluding some more of the lovely graffiti on the right. The positioning of your red sunburst is good.<br/> <br/>Nicely done, thanks for your submission and apologies for the delayed critique, as they say, ''better late than never'', or at least I hope it is, Sid</td>', '*Hello from Sid and the Critique Club* An interesting image that fulfils the challenge perfectly. There can be no mistake that this most certainly is a huge lump of concrete, but not just any old concrete, it is a very important part of recent history. It is brought alive by the colourful graffiti and your title during the challenge and post challenge with your very helpful comments. I know one of your commenters likes the inclusion of the background, I am not so keen, or rather I love the irregular left edge of the concrete and this should be preserved by the inclusion of a minimal background, I think where the brown structure becomes black would have been the ideal amount to include and I would have dodged or cloned the part of the canopy that would intrude to make it more uniform. The bright lights of bokeh in the window are very distracting and spoil the overall result especially as we are excluding some more of the lovely graffiti on the right. The positioning of your red sunburst is good. Nicely done, thanks for your submission and apologies for the delayed critique, as they say, ''better late than never'', or at least I hope it is, Sid', '2015-09-03 09:36:01.000000', NULL, 0);
COMMIT;
